WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:04.440
I take a look at saving
some costs by using

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.090
a different network tier.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.922
Will our performance stack up?

00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:08.880
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.716
If you take a hard look at your
application's network data,

00:00:14.716 --> 00:00:16.590
you'll find that it's
not consistent in terms

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.220
of priority.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.340
I mean, it's really nice that
your app can use a two terabyte

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.052
connection for video,
but do you really

00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:24.510
need that much performance when
uploading your log content?

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.430
Or better yet,
does it make sense

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.400
to pay for that performance
when uploading your log content?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.759
This is where Google
Cloud's new Standard Tier

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:33.960
networking comes in.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.170
It offers lower throughput,
higher latency, and lower cost

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.910
than the default Premium Tier.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.410
The difference
between the two tiers

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.890
has to do with how long packets
stay on the public internet.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.140
Now, see, in the
Premium Tier, packets

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.350
enter Google's networks as
close to the user as possible

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.030
and then travel on
Google's backbones

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.440
before getting to the VM.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.410
The Standard Tier,
on the other hand,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.910
allows packets to enter Google's
networks as close to the VM

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.950
as possible, spending
more time traveling

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.330
on the public internet.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.580
And since it's spending less
time on Google's network,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.800
it costs less as a result.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.590
Now, as a developer,
there's two big questions.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.120
How much performance
am I losing,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.956
and how much money am I saving?

00:01:10.956 --> 00:01:12.330
To figure out the
first question,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.220
let's take a look at
the worst case scenario,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.260
transferring data
across an ocean.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.590
I set up two VMs in US central
one and configured one of them

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.980
for the Premium Tier and the
other for the Standard Tier.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.070
Then from a non-GCP
machine in the UK,

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.330
fetched a 100k image from
each instance about 500 times.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.660
The graph here shows the
Premium Tier is about 41%

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.670
faster on average than
the Standard Tier.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.470
Now, we can also run this test
in the best case scenario,

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.760
where the client and the
server are geographically

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.020
closer to each other.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.550
In this configuration,
we can see

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.500
that the Standard
Tier is only about 8%

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.580
slower than the Premium Tier.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.510
Now, at the time of this
video, GCP's tiered network

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.840
is still in alpha, so lots
of stuff is still in flux.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.550
As such, keep an eye
on the official site

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.597
for more information.

00:01:57.597 --> 00:01:59.430
And if you want to know
more about our tests

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.440
or how you could use the
Standard Tier to save

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.090
your app some money,
check out the article

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.840
linked in the description
below, and don't

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.560
forget to check out the rest
of our Cloud Performance Atlas

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.339
content.

00:02:08.339 --> 00:02:09.880
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.130
every millisecond counts.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

